 .$ However, note that this point is not included in the graph.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $-\\infty $ and $1,$ but not including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $-\\infty <y<1,$ which can also be written as  $-\\infty <f(x)<1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490103 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1069 "q-236388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/49/49288e2c082854cfc27a2d48af2d82a96016a3c1394066471d49873e4c75878e.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?\n\nThe range is \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c426db9-03a0-51ed-9522-bdc02ff647af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490228 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $1.$ This occurs when $x=0.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $1$ and $\\infty ,$ including $1.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $1\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as \n$1\\le f(x)<\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186490119 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-129372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490231 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/eb/eb292b77908dbb4590b6bbc49c452215edd8e6668e003e0984074a7ca22bf4ff.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30dbc182-568e-5645-9ba2-4447169ef008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186490231 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the 